A||N||10||sailordude||Fiero Problems||||9||
Z||000000||sailordude||06-02-2001||08:46 PM||||Hi guys, I'm having a problem with my car and I don't know what it is.  The engine sounds like it is running fine, however I put it into first gear, start driving, it goes pretty good off the get go, then gets up to speed and basically stops accelerating.  So i put it into second, and it will chug along.  It almost feels like it is running out of gas, but that is not the case.  Has anyone else had this problem? If so what was it, and how did you fix it?<P>Thanks guys,||||reg||9
Z||000001||87GTZ34||06-02-2001||08:54 PM||||Sounds like it could be plugged exhaust?!?  Did this start suddenly?||||reg||1
Z||000002||sailordude||06-02-2001||08:58 PM||||Well i was driving down the highway today, came to a slight hill, and i found the car was pulling back,  with in a few miles i was only going about 50 and still slowing down.  So I pull off on the nearest exit, pulled into a gas station, shut the car off, waited a bit and tried it again.  That's when all the stuff happened that i mentioned before.  So i don't know what to do.  I ended up having to pay $100 to get it towed home.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||06-02-2001||09:05 PM||||Sounds like the catalytic converter.<P>Lack of power without a misfire is usually air, fuel, or exhaust related.  <P>So other possibilities include the fuel filter and air filter.  <P>And yes, I've had that problem.  Fuel filter one time ('76 Toyota), catalytic converter twice (Fiero).  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||sailordude||06-02-2001||09:08 PM||||can that be repaired or does it have to be replaced?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jefrysuko||06-02-2001||09:22 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sailordude:<BR><B>can that be repaired or does it have to be replaced?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P>He has the best catalytic converter replacements I have been able to find.  That is if you still have your stock exhaust!<p>[This message has been edited by Jefrysuko (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||06-02-2001||10:07 PM||||I was having similar problems with my GT, turned out one of my sparkplug wires had a hole burnt in the sleeve, and was arcing against a part near the coil. I repaired it by putting a small piece of wire loom filled with silicone sealant over the burnt part. Runs great now (knocking on wood loudly).||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||06-02-2001||10:58 PM||||Put a vacuum gauge on it and rev (snap the throttle) the engine up to 5000-rpm. If the vacuum drops and them climbs back up very slowly or even drops down than you have a clogged exhaust. If the vacuum drops and then recovers quickly than it's normal.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||maryjane||06-03-2001||10:29 AM||||88--Hope this doesn't end up a double post...<BR>Does that vacumn test work on a car with a carb and no ecm. I have a Jeep I suspect of having a plugged cat.  Thanks in advance.<BR>Don||||reg||5
Z||000009||88formula||06-03-2001||12:00 PM||||It works on any internal combustion engine regardless of the fuel delivery system except diesels.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||sailordude||06-05-2001||12:17 PM||||Thanks for all the help guys...it ended up being a clogged cat converter.  Oh well...she purrrrrrrssssssss....like a kitten now.<P>thanks again,<P>Catch ya on the flip side!||||reg||1
